<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Biophys Biochem Cytol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Biophysical and Biochemical Cytology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0095-9901</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">13357506</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2229747</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>INTRACISTERNAL GRANULES IN THE EXOCRINE CELLS OF THE PANCREAS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Palade</surname><given-names>George E.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x3995200N0x3431550">From The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>25</day><month>7</month><year>1956</year></pub-date><volume>2</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>417</fpage><lpage>422</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>10</day><month>5</month><year>1956</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1956, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Dense, homogeneous granules of 250 to 350 m&#x000b5; in diameter have been found inside the cavities of the endoplasmic reticulum in the acinar cells of the pancreas of the guinea pig. They apparently correspond to the fine granules described in the same material in light microscopy by Mankowski and Bensley. The location of these granules indicates that the endoplasmic reticulum is a cavitary system and suggests its participation in secretory processes. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>